# Lecture 10D

## Chromosome Aneuploidy

## Chapter 9

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 10, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish polyploidy, aneuploidy, trisomy, and monosomy.
- Predict gametes after nondisjunction in meiosis I or II.
- Relate trisomy 21 to chromosome segregation.
- Compare prenatal screening with diagnostic chromosome testing.

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Aneuploidy

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chromosome Alterations*

@body

- Large-scale chromosomal alterations in humans and other mammals often lead to spontaneous abortions (miscarriages) or cause a variety of developmental disorders
- Plants tolerate such genetic changes better than animals do

=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


@top-left

- About 15% of all pregnancies terminate in spontaneous abortion.
- many (\~50%) due to chromosomal abnormalities

@bottom-left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_002.png)

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_001.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosome Number and DNA Content

@body

| Question | Terms |
| --- | --- |
| Complete chromosome sets? | *Euploid*: whole sets; *polyploid*: more than two sets |
| Extra or missing individual chromosomes? | *Aneuploid* |
| DNA gained or lost in a rearrangement? | *Unbalanced* if gained/lost; *balanced* if neither |

These are different questions. Count chromosomes *and* track their DNA.

We will work through deletions and translocations in Lecture 11.

=== layout: section

# Polyploid

=== layout: two-panels

# Polyploid: More Than Two Complete Sets

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_003.png)

@right

- *Diploid*: two complete sets
- *Triploid*: three complete sets
- *Tetraploid*: four complete sets
- Polyploidy is especially common in plants
- A polyploid may not produce fertile offspring with its diploid ancestor

=== layout: one-panel

# How Can a Cell Become Tetraploid?

@body

- Start with a *diploid* cell: two complete chromosome sets.
- DNA is copied, but cell division does not finish.
- The cell can keep the copies intended for *both daughter cells*.

| Outcome | Chromosome Sets |
| --- | --- |
| Division finishes | Two daughter cells, two sets each |
| Division fails | One cell can retain four sets: *tetraploid* |

A human example, with all four sets in one nucleus: *46,XY* becomes *92,XXYY*.

*DNA copying alone does not make a cell tetraploid.* A normally replicated diploid cell has sister chromatids.

@citation

https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1002679

@notes

Follow the whole chromosome complement, not an extra copy of just one chromosome.
Failure of cell separation can initially leave two diploid nuclei in one cell. The
92,XXYY example describes a tetraploid complement in one nucleus, rather than every
possible failed division. A normal replicated diploid cell still has 46 chromosomes,
each with two sister chromatids; DNA replication alone does not change its ploidy.

=== layout: one-panel

# One Basic Set or One Gamete?

@body

| Quantity | What Are We Counting? |
| --- | --- |
| *Monoploid, m* | Chromosomes in one basic set |
| *Haploid, h* | Chromosomes in a gamete after regular reduction |

- Diploid: one gamete has one basic set
- Octoploid: one gamete has *four* basic sets
- These numbers need not be equal in a polyploid

=== layout: one-panel

# Practice: One Basic Set and One Gamete

@body

An octoploid plant has *64 chromosomes* in a somatic cell.

What are its monoploid (*m*) and haploid (*h*) numbers?

| Choice | m | h |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | 8 | 8 |
| B | 32 | 32 |
| C | 8 | 32 |
| D | 32 | 8 |
| E | 64 | 32 |
| F | 64 | 8 |

Hint: 64 &divide; 8 = 8; 64 &divide; 2 = 32. Assume regular reduction in meiosis.

=== layout: one-panel

# Count Sets First, Then Gametes

@body

| Quantity | Calculation | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One basic set | 64 &divide; 8 | [*m = 8*]{color=#076DAD} |
| One gamete | Half of 64 chromosomes | [*h = 32*]{color=#B74300} |

- [*Answer: C*]{color=#007A00}
- A gamete still contains *four basic sets*
- The homework writes octoploid as 8n; here, eight refers to the number of basic sets

=== layout: one-panel

# Practice: Chromosomes in a Gamete

@body

An octoploid plant has *72 chromosomes* in a somatic cell. How many chromosomes would be present in its gametes after regular reduction?

- A. 4
- B. 9
- C. 16
- D. 18
- E. 36

Hint: 72 &divide; 2 = 36; 72 &divide; 8 = 9.

=== layout: one-panel

# Gamete Count: Answer

@body

- Meiosis reduces the chromosome count by half
- 72 &divide; 2 = [*36 chromosomes per gamete*]{color=#007A00}
- [*Answer: E*]{color=#007A00}
- 72 &divide; 8 = 9 is the number in *one basic set*
- Four basic sets per gamete: 4 &times; 9 = 36

=== layout: section

# Aneuploid

=== layout: two-panels

# Extra or Missing Chromosomes

@left

- [*Trisomy*]{color=#003FFF}: three copies of one chromosome
- [*Monosomy*]{color=#D40000}: one copy of one chromosome
- The change affects an individual chromosome, rather than a whole set

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_004.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Find the Extra Chromosome

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_005.png)

*Which chromosome has three copies?*

=== layout: one-panel

# Read the Extra Copy

@body

| Karyotype Part | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 47 | Total chromosome count |
| XY | Sex-chromosome complement |
| +21 | Extra copy of chromosome 21 |

The extra chromosome in the photograph is the *+21* in *47,XY,+21*.

DNA replication changes chromatids, not this chromosome count.

=== layout: section

# Nondisjunction

=== layout: one-panel

# What Failed to Separate?

@body

| Division | Normal Separation | Nondisjunction |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Meiosis I | Homologous chromosomes | Homologs go to the same cell |
| Meiosis II | Sister chromatids | Sisters go to the same cell |

Follow *one chromosome pair* first. The other chromosomes can separate normally.

=== layout: one-panel

# Nondisjunction in Meiosis I

@body

![Nondisjunction in Meiosis I](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_meiosis_i.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Nondisjunction in Meiosis II

@body

![Nondisjunction in Meiosis II](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_meiosis_ii.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Follow the Four Gametes

@body

![Follow the Four Gametes](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_gametes.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Check the Four Products

@body

| Error | Gametes | What Changed? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Meiosis I | n + 1, n + 1, n - 1, n - 1 | All four have the wrong count |
| Meiosis II in one cell | n + 1, n - 1, n, n | Two have the normal count |

Count chromosomes in *all four products* before choosing an answer.

=== layout: one-panel

# Add a Normal Gamete

@body

| Gamete After an Error | Normal Gamete | Zygote | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| n - 1 | n | [*2n - 1*]{color=#D40000} | Monosomy |
| n + 1 | n | [*2n + 1*]{color=#003FFF} | Trisomy |

- Gamete notation: *n - 1* or *n + 1*
- Diploid zygote notation: *2n - 1* or *2n + 1*
- For humans: 22 + 23 = 45; 24 + 23 = 47

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

If cell X enters meiosis, and nondisjunction of one chromosome occurs in one of its daughter cells during meiosis II, what will be the result at the completion of meiosis?

- A) All the gametes descended from cell X will be diploid.
- B) Half of the gametes descended from cell X will be n + 1, and half will be n - 1.
- C) 1/4 of the gametes descended from cell X will be n + 1, 1/4 will be n - 1, and 1/2 will be n.
- D) Two of the four gametes descended from cell X will be haploid, and two will be diploid.

@answer

[Answer\: C]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# Try It: Follow the X Chromosome

@body

A father has X-linked recessive color blindness. The mother has two normal color-vision alleles. Their daughter has *45,X* and is color-blind.

Which parent produced the gamete missing a sex chromosome?

- A. Mother; the daughter received her X from her father
- B. Father; the daughter received her X from her mother
- C. Cannot be determined

Assume no new mutation or chromosome loss after fertilization.

=== layout: one-panel

# Turner Syndrome: Follow the Allele

@body

- The daughter has one X chromosome
- Her color-blindness allele came from her father
- Her father contributed an X-bearing sperm
- Her mother contributed an egg without an X
- [*Answer: A*]{color=#007A00}

This identifies the parent in this problem; it does not distinguish meiosis I from meiosis II.

=== layout: one-panel

# Three Common Autosomal Trisomies

@body

| Extra Chromosome | Syndrome | Examples of Effects |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 13 | *Patau* | Brain and heart defects; cleft lip/palate |
| 18 | *Edwards* | Low birthweight; organ defects; clenched fists |
| 21 | *Down* | Developmental delay; variable health effects |

- Trisomy 13 and 18: many affected infants die early in life
- Trisomy 21: survival into adulthood is common

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/trisomy-13/  https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/trisomy-18/

