=== layout: subsection

# Can People with Down Syndrome Have Children?

=== layout: one-panel

# Fertility and Chromosome Segregation

@body

- Women with Down syndrome can and do have children
- Fertility is usually reduced in men; rare paternity reports exist
- Now ask a simpler chromosome question:
  - How can *three homologs* separate during meiosis?
  - What gametes would that produce?

@citation

https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17094988/

=== layout: one-panel

# A Simplified Segregation Problem

@body

Assume a trisomic cell produces two equally common gamete types:

- One copy of chromosome 21
- Two copies of chromosome 21

Each gamete joins a normal gamete with one copy. Assume equal fertilization and survival.

*What fraction of the resulting zygotes would have trisomy 21?*

This is a chromosome model, not an estimate of live-birth risk.

=== layout: subsection

# How Do Three Homologs Separate?

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_027.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Two Copies Go One Way, One the Other

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_028.png)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_029.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# The Model Gives a 1:1 Ratio

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_030.png)

@right

- Half the gametes have one copy
- Half have two copies
- Add one copy from a normal gamete
- Half the zygotes have two copies; half have three
- [*Model answer: 50% trisomy*]{color=#007A00}

Actual reproductive outcomes also depend on fertilization and survival.

=== layout: section

# Prenatal Testing

=== layout: one-panel

# Screening or Diagnosis?

@body

| Screening | Diagnostic Testing |
| --- | --- |
| Estimates the chance of a condition | Tests for a condition in sampled cells or DNA |
| Blood tests and ultrasound | For example, chromosome testing after CVS or amniocentesis |
| Can have false positives and false negatives | Answers the question addressed by that laboratory test |

A positive screen is a reason to discuss further testing, not a diagnosis.

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/diagnosis/screening-for-birth-defects.html

=== layout: one-panel

# Prenatal Blood Screening

@body

- Blood screening estimates the chance of a condition
- A triple screen measures *AFP*, *hCG*, and *estriol*
- A quad screen also measures *inhibin-A*
- An unusual result can occur in a pregnancy without the condition
- A screening result is not a diagnosis

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/diagnosis/screening-for-birth-defects.html

=== layout: two-panels

# Nuchal Translucency Scan

@left

- Ultrasound looks for extra fluid behind the fetal neck
- Used with blood tests in first-trimester screening
- Estimates likelihood; does not establish a diagnosis
- An abnormal screen can lead to an offer of diagnostic testing

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_017.png)

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_018.png)

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/diagnosis/screening-for-birth-defects.html

=== layout: one-panel

# Diagnostic Samples

@body

| Sample | Where It Comes From |
| --- | --- |
| [*Amniocentesis*]{color=#D40000} | Amniotic fluid containing fetal cells |
| [*Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS)*]{color=#007A00} | Placental tissue |
| [*Umbilical Blood Sampling (PUBS)*]{color=#B74300} | Fetal blood from the umbilical cord |

The sample provides cells or DNA. The laboratory test determines what can be detected.

